Theorem G follows from (P1) and unimodality. The same construction works at every edge density, including \(\lambda_Q<\Lambda\). It changes only the trial nodes beyond \(Q\).

Let \(\ell\) be the width of the last continuum cell, so that \(L\int_{Q-\ell}^Q r_Q=1\), and put \(d=\max(\eta,\ell)\). The construction places a fixed number \(M\) of exterior nodes at \(Q+iAd\), \(1\le i\le M\). At an interior node one cell or more from \(Q\), their combined gain can exceed \(C_S\eta/\rho(y)\) by taking \(M\) large. The issue is whether these exterior nodes satisfy their own Bethe inequalities. They do because the continuum density at depths between \(d\) and \(iAd\) supplies a logarithmic lower bound on \(t(Q+iAd)-n\). Taking \(A\) large controls the coefficient of the competing logarithm from the exterior nodes to the left.

Here is the proof, including the case in which \(Q\) is too small to fit the interval used in that estimate.

## 1. The inequality for arbitrary exterior positions

Write \(k=K_0\), \(\kappa=k+\tfrac12\), and suppose \(n>k\). Keep the \(n-k\) interior trial nodes at
\[
t(y_j)=j+k,\qquad 1\le j\le n-k.
\]
In particular, \(y_{n-k}=Q\). Place \(m\le k\) further nodes at arbitrary ordered points \(z_1<\cdots<z_m\) above \(Q\), and the remaining \(k-m\) nodes at \(+\infty\).

For each interior node \(y=y_j\), the following lower bound holds:
\[
\frac{\widetilde F_j}{2\pi}
\ge
(1-q)\kappa+\kappa B(Q+y)+\frac12 B(Q-y)
+\sum_{i=1}^m B(z_i-y)-S(y).
\tag{1}
\]

To see this, let \(d\mu(v)=Lr_Q(v)\mathbf 1_{[-Q,Q]}(v)\,dv\). Differentiating in \(u\) and evaluating at \(u=0\) gives
\[
\frac{Lp(u)}{2\pi}+\int\Theta(u-v)\,d\mu(v)=t(u)-\frac n2.
\tag{2}
\]
Both derivatives equal \(Ls(u)\), and both sides vanish at zero by symmetry. Let
\[
D(v)=L N_0(v)-\#\{j:y_j\le v\},
\]
where only finite nodes contribute at finite \(v\). Integration by parts in (2), using \(\Theta(-\infty)=-q/2\) for the nodes at infinity, gives
\[
\frac{\widetilde F_j}{2\pi}
=t(y_j)-\frac n2-I_j-\int_{\mathbb R}T(y_j-v)D(v)\,dv.
\tag{3}
\]
Indeed, the boundary term from \(D(+\infty)=k-m\) cancels the contribution of the \(k-m\) infinite nodes.

At an interior node the first three terms in (3) give \(\kappa\). On \([-Q,Q]\),
\[
D(v)=t(v)-\max(0,\lfloor t(v)\rfloor-k)
\le k+\{t(v)\}=\kappa+s^\#(t(v)).
\]
Below \(-Q\), \(D=0\). Above \(Q\),
\[
D(v)=k-\sum_{i=1}^m\mathbf 1_{\{v\ge z_i\}}.
\]
Substitution in (3) proves (1), since \(t(y_j)\) is an integer. In particular, the gain from one exterior node is its full tail \(B(z_i-y)\).

We will use two elementary kernel estimates with universal \(b,c>0\):
\[
 b\frac{\eta}{x}\le B(x)\le\frac{\eta}{\pi x}
 \quad(\eta\le x\le2),\qquad
 (1-q)+B(x)\ge c\frac{\eta}{\min(1,\eta+x)}
 \quad(x\ge0).
\tag{4}
\]
The upper bound follows by integrating \(T(w)\le\eta/(\pi w^2)\), and holds for all \(x>0\). For the lower bound, the explicit kernel gives \(T(w)\ge c_1\eta/w^2\) for \(\eta\le w\le4\). Integrate over \([x,2x]\). For the second inequality use \(B(x)\ge B(\eta)\) when \(x<\eta\), the first estimate when \(\eta\le x\le1\), and \(1-q=2\eta/\pi\) when \(x\ge1\).

## 2. The exterior nodes satisfy their inequalities

The constants will be chosen in the order \(A,M,k\), with \(k\ge M\) and \(k\ge2\). Since \(n>k\), the last cell lies in the right half of the interval. Define
\[
 L\int_{Q-\ell}^Q r_Q(v)\,dv=1,\qquad
 d=\max(\eta,\ell),\qquad H=Ad,\qquad R=\min(1,Q).
\]
First suppose
\[
 MH\le R/4.
\tag{5}
\]
Use \(m=M\) exterior nodes at \(z_i=Q+iH\).

By unimodality, the density at the inner end of the last cell is at least its average. Moving farther inward up to the origin only increases it. Thus
\[
 Lr_Q(Q-u)\ge \frac1\ell\ge\frac1d
 \qquad (\ell\le u\le Q).
\tag{6}
\]

Fix \(i\), write \(x=iH\), and let \(E_i=t(Q+x)-n\). Positivity of \(a_1\), the equation for \(s\), and (6) give
\[
\begin{aligned}
 E_i
 &=L\int_0^x s(Q+w)\,dw\\
 &\ge \int_d^x Lr_Q(Q-u)\int_0^u T(u+w)\,dw\,du\\
 &\ge c_2\frac{\eta}{d}\int_d^x\frac{du}{u}
 =c_2\frac{\eta}{d}\log(Ai).
\end{aligned}
\tag{7}
\]
The integration region is admissible because \(x\le Q/4\). On it, \(\eta\le u\le u+w\le2u\le1/2\), so \(T(u+w)\ge c_1\eta/(4u^2)\). This proves (7) with a universal \(c_2>0\).

Now take the Bethe inequality at \(z_i\), whose index is \(j=n-k+i\). For a finite ordered node, writing \(\Theta(w)=q/2-B(w)\) at positive separations gives
\[
 \frac{\widetilde F_j}{2\pi}
 =\frac{Lp(z_i)}{2\pi}-(1-q)I_j
 -\sum_{a<j}B(z_i-y_a)+\sum_{a>j}B(y_a-z_i),
\tag{8}
\]
with \(B(+\infty)=0\). The constant sign contributions sum to \(qI_j\). Since \(z_i>Q\), (2) also gives
\[
 \frac{Lp(z_i)}{2\pi}
 =E_i+(1-q)\frac n2+\int B(z_i-v)\,d\mu(v).
\tag{9}
\]

The discrete sum over the interior nodes exceeds the integral in (9) by at most \(B(iH)\). Indeed, split \([-Q,Q]\) into all \(n\) unit-\(\mu\)-mass cells. The integrand \(g(v)=B(z_i-v)\) is increasing. On each cell its right-endpoint value exceeds its integral by at most the increment of \(g\) across that cell. Summing gives at most \(g(Q)-g(-Q)\le B(iH)\). Our trial omits the first \(k\) right endpoints, which only decreases the discrete sum.

In (8), drop the nonnegative terms from nodes to the right and use \(n/2-I_j=k-i+\tfrac12>0\). Equations (7)–(9) then give
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \frac{\widetilde F_j}{2\pi}
 &\ge E_i-B(iH)-\sum_{l=1}^{i-1}B(lH)\\
 &\ge \frac{\eta}{d}
 \left[c_2\log(Ai)-\frac{1+\log i}{\pi A}\right].
\end{aligned}
\tag{10}
\]
Choose \(A\ge e\) with \(1/(\pi A)\le c_2\). The bracket is nonnegative for every \(i\ge1\), independently of \(M\). This checks the top finite node as well, where there is no gain from a node to its right.

## 3. The exterior gain absorbs (P1)

Continue to assume (5). For an interior node \(0<y<Q\), write \(\delta=Q-y\). Since \(t(Q)-t(y)\) is a positive integer, \(\delta\ge\ell\). If \(\delta<1\), then
\[
 \rho(y)=\min(1,\eta+\delta)\ge\delta,\qquad \rho(y)\ge d.
\tag{11}
\]
The second inequality uses \(d\le1\), which follows from (5). Also \(\eta\le\delta+iH\le 1+1/4<2\). Thus (4) and (11) give
\[
 \sum_{i=1}^M B(z_i-y)
 \ge b\eta\sum_{i=1}^M\frac1{\delta+iAd}
 \ge \frac{b\eta}{\rho(y)}\sum_{i=1}^M\frac1{1+iA}.
\tag{12}
\]
With \(A\) already fixed, choose a finite universal \(M\) so that the last sum times \(b\) is at least \(C_S\). By (P1), the exterior gain in (12) covers \(S(y)\) in (1). This includes the node one cell from \(Q\).

The node at \(Q\) has no cell to its right. Directly from the sawtooth integral,
\[
 S(Q)\le \frac12\int_{-Q}^Q T(Q-v)\,dv
 \le \frac12 B(0).
\tag{13}
\]
This is already covered by the third term of (1). For \(y\le0\), one has \(\rho(y)=\min(1,\eta+Q+y)\), so (4) and (P1) give the required bound from \(\kappa[(1-q)+B(Q+y)]\) once \(k\) is universally large. For \(y>0\) with \(\delta\ge1\), \(\rho(y)=1\), and \((1-q)\kappa\) suffices.

## 4. When the interval is too short

Suppose now that (5) fails, so
\[
 R<4AMd.
\tag{14}
\]
Put all \(k\) exterior nodes at \(+\infty\), that is, set \(m=0\) in (1). The arguments for \(y\le0\), \(\delta\ge1\), and \(y=Q\) still apply.

At a remaining node \(0<y<Q\) with \(\delta<1\), the integer-cell observation again gives \(\delta\ge\ell\), hence \(\rho(y)\ge\min(1,d)\). Set \(\sigma=\min(1,\eta+Q+y)\). Since \(Q+y\le2Q\), (14) yields
\[
 \sigma\le \min(1,\eta+2R)
 \le (1+8AM)\min(1,d)
 \le (1+8AM)\rho(y).
\tag{15}
\]
Consequently, by (4),
\[
 \kappa[(1-q)+B(Q+y)]
 \ge \frac{c\kappa}{1+8AM}\frac{\eta}{\rho(y)}.
\tag{16}
\]
Choose \(k\ge M\) large enough that \(c\kappa\ge(1+8AM)C_S\), also increasing it to meet the requirements of Section 3 above. Then (16) covers (P1). This completes the choice of constants without any dependence on \(Q,\eta,L,n\).

## 5. Counting and reflection

Every finite node of the chosen trial satisfies \(\widetilde F_j\ge0\). Comparison gives \(x_j^*\le y_j\) at each such node. On \([-Q,Q]\) the trial count is \(\max(0,\lfloor t(v)\rfloor-k)\), and at or above \(Q\) it is at least \(n-k\). Hence, for all \(v\),
\[
 N_x(v)\ge N_y(v)\ge N_0(v)-\frac{k+1}{L}.
\]
Continuity of \(N_0\) gives the same lower bound for the left limit \(N_x(v-)\). Using \(N_x(v)=\nu-N_x((-v)-)\) and \(N_0(v)=\nu-N_0(-v)\) gives the upper bound. If \(n\le k\), both counting functions lie in \([0,n/L]\), so the result holds there as well. Thus Theorem G holds with
\[
 \boxed{\displaystyle K=k+1,\qquad \eta_0=1/64.}
\]

One correction to the supplied facts is worth recording. The density-ratio sentence in (F3), as written, fails when \(\rho\) saturates at \(1\). For example, two points on the same side with \(\rho(v)=\rho(y)=1\) would be required to have equal densities. On such an interval \(a_1\) strictly decreases on the positive side and the convolution of the even unimodal \(T\) and \(r_Q\mathbf1_{[-Q,Q]}\) is nonincreasing, so \(r_Q\) strictly decreases there. The derivative estimate gives the valid bound
\[
 \left|\log\frac{r_Q(v)}{r_Q(y)}\right|
 \le C\left|\int_y^v\frac{du}{\rho(u)}\right|.
\]
The proof above uses (P1) as supplied and never uses the density-ratio sentence. Also, for \(k>0\), the displayed criterion (**) is sufficient as stated. Calling it “exact” overlooks the favorable slack from the first \(k\) cells in the bound on \(D\) leading to (1).

